\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The game and the design.} (\textbf  {a}) The stage game at $\lambda = 1$. Cells are penalties the agent is told to minimise, so $T<R<P<S$ and Option A is the dominant, defecting action. Greed and fear are computed on the utility scale $u = -\text  {penalty}$. Every cell is multiplied by $\lambda \in \{0.1,1,10\}$, a positive affine rescaling that leaves the game strategically identical. (\textbf  {b}) The fully crossed design: 2{,}400 dyads, 48{,}000 binary decisions, with 40 dyads in every model $\times $ scale $\times $ language cell. The horizon row is shaded as a confound rather than a factor, because only the Gemini arm was told the round count.}}{8}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:design}{{1}{8}{\textbf {The game and the design.} (\textbf {a}) The stage game at $\lam = 1$. Cells are penalties the agent is told to minimise, so $T<R<P<S$ and Option A is the dominant, defecting action. Greed and fear are computed on the utility scale $u = -\text {penalty}$. Every cell is multiplied by $\lam \in \{0.1,1,10\}$, a positive affine rescaling that leaves the game strategically identical. (\textbf {b}) The fully crossed design: 2{,}400 dyads, 48{,}000 binary decisions, with 40 dyads in every model $\times $ scale $\times $ language cell. The horizon row is shaded as a confound rather than a factor, because only the Gemini arm was told the round count}{figure.caption.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {What the pooled cooperation rate hides.} (\textbf  {a}) The distribution of within-game cooperation. Ten rounds admit only eleven values, so this is the discrete distribution, not a histogram. Each row is normalised to its own maximum, so shapes are comparable but heights are not counts. The vertical rule marks the pooled mean, drawn through the distribution it is meant to summarise; the coloured percentages give the share of games played entirely one way. (\textbf  {b}) Variance decomposition of the agent-game cooperation rate. The top four rows are sequential (Type I) main effects entered in the order shown, so each is credited only with what the rows above leave unexplained, which makes model identity's 9.5\% an upper bound. The fifth row is everything the same four factors explain jointly that they do not explain additively. The last two rows lie outside the four crossed factors: which seat of the dyad an agent occupies, which the design fixes but does not manipulate, and the spread across the ten replicates of one byte-identical prompt. (\textbf  {c}) What that last row is made of. Bars give the standard deviation of cooperation across the ten replicates, against the floor that ten independent rounds would already impose; the annotation is their ratio. The floor is 42\% of the replicate variance, so the row is neither pure arithmetic nor pure caprice.}}{9}{figure.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:aggregate}{{2}{9}{\textbf {What the pooled cooperation rate hides.} (\textbf {a}) The distribution of within-game cooperation. Ten rounds admit only eleven values, so this is the discrete distribution, not a histogram. Each row is normalised to its own maximum, so shapes are comparable but heights are not counts. The vertical rule marks the pooled mean, drawn through the distribution it is meant to summarise; the coloured percentages give the share of games played entirely one way. (\textbf {b}) Variance decomposition of the agent-game cooperation rate. The top four rows are sequential (Type I) main effects entered in the order shown, so each is credited only with what the rows above leave unexplained, which makes model identity's 9.5\% an upper bound. The fifth row is everything the same four factors explain jointly that they do not explain additively. The last two rows lie outside the four crossed factors: which seat of the dyad an agent occupies, which the design fixes but does not manipulate, and the spread across the ten replicates of one byte-identical prompt. (\textbf {c}) What that last row is made of. Bars give the standard deviation of cooperation across the ten replicates, against the floor that ten independent rounds would already impose; the annotation is their ratio. The floor is 42\% of the replicate variance, so the row is neither pure arithmetic nor pure caprice}{figure.caption.5}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {3.2}Payoff magnitude changes behaviour}{9}{subsection.3.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:lambda}{{3.2}{9}{Payoff magnitude changes behaviour}{subsection.3.2}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Behavioural profile of each model.} Every model contributes 600 dyads, 1{,}200 agent-games and 12{,}000 decisions; cooperation is pooled over the whole design. ``Scale step'' is the change in cooperation from $\lambda = 0.1$ to $\lambda = 1$ with 95\% bootstrap CI, the contrast that carries the entire payoff-scale effect; the step from $\lambda = 1$ to $\lambda = 10$ is consistent with zero in all four models (\S  \ref {sec:lambda}) and its null, like this one's, is exactly zero. The language gap is measured within the three languages sharing one objective framing. ``Opening C'' gives $p$(cooperate on round 1) for an agent told it is cooperative and for one told it is selfish; the ordering is reversed in every model. Distance is Euclidean, in the four-dimensional memory-one fingerprint space, to the nearest of the five canonical rules, which are themselves at most 2.0 apart. ``Exact rule'' is the share of agent-games in which exactly one of the \emph  {four} exact-match rules reproduces the whole trajectory; grim trigger enters the distance but not the match, at the cost given in \S  \ref {sec:strategy}, and the wider 32-rule vocabulary is treated in \S  \ref {sec:residual}. The last two columns are read-out statistics and are not comparable across models, because sampling temperature is confounded with model identity (\S  \ref {sec:confounds}).}}{10}{table.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:profile}{{1}{10}{\textbf {Behavioural profile of each model.} Every model contributes 600 dyads, 1{,}200 agent-games and 12{,}000 decisions; cooperation is pooled over the whole design. ``Scale step'' is the change in cooperation from $\lam = 0.1$ to $\lam = 1$ with 95\% bootstrap CI, the contrast that carries the entire payoff-scale effect; the step from $\lam = 1$ to $\lam = 10$ is consistent with zero in all four models (\S \ref {sec:lambda}) and its null, like this one's, is exactly zero. The language gap is measured within the three languages sharing one objective framing. ``Opening C'' gives $p$(cooperate on round 1) for an agent told it is cooperative and for one told it is selfish; the ordering is reversed in every model. Distance is Euclidean, in the four-dimensional memory-one fingerprint space, to the nearest of the five canonical rules, which are themselves at most 2.0 apart. ``Exact rule'' is the share of agent-games in which exactly one of the \emph {four} exact-match rules reproduces the whole trajectory; grim trigger enters the distance but not the match, at the cost given in \S \ref {sec:strategy}, and the wider 32-rule vocabulary is treated in \S \ref {sec:residual}. The last two columns are read-out statistics and are not comparable across models, because sampling temperature is confounded with model identity (\S \ref {sec:confounds})}{table.caption.6}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {3.3}Prompt language changes behaviour, with framing held fixed}{10}{subsection.3.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:language}{{3.3}{10}{Prompt language changes behaviour, with framing held fixed}{subsection.3.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Three manipulations that cannot matter, and all three do.} All intervals are 95\% bootstrap CIs resampling whole dyads. (\textbf  {a}) Change in cooperation between adjacent payoff scales. A positive affine rescaling leaves the game strategically identical, so both nulls are exactly zero. Three of four models move on the step from $\times 0.1$ to $\times 1$ (filled); none moves on the step from $\times 1$ to $\times 10$ (open), although it is the larger change in magnitude by a factor of ten. The effect is a step, not a slope. (\textbf  {b}) Language disparity, the gap between a model's highest- and lowest-cooperation language. Pale bars use all five languages, in which objective framing varies with language; solid bars use only English, French and Vietnamese, which share a single framing. Dashes mark the 95th percentile of a permutation null, which is also the smallest disparity the test could have detected; $P$ values are for the framing-controlled comparison. (\textbf  {c}) Effect of the assigned persona, resolved by payoff scale: the difference between an agent told it is cooperative and one told it is selfish, at each $\lambda $. At $\lambda = 0.1$ every model lies left of zero, the instruction moving behaviour opposite to its semantic content. At $\lambda \ge 1$ two of the four cross to the right of it. A manipulation that cannot matter decides the sign of one that could, so neither the pooled effect nor any single scale is the whole result.}}{11}{figure.caption.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:invariance}{{3}{11}{\textbf {Three manipulations that cannot matter, and all three do.} All intervals are 95\% bootstrap CIs resampling whole dyads. (\textbf {a}) Change in cooperation between adjacent payoff scales. A positive affine rescaling leaves the game strategically identical, so both nulls are exactly zero. Three of four models move on the step from $\times 0.1$ to $\times 1$ (filled); none moves on the step from $\times 1$ to $\times 10$ (open), although it is the larger change in magnitude by a factor of ten. The effect is a step, not a slope. (\textbf {b}) Language disparity, the gap between a model's highest- and lowest-cooperation language. Pale bars use all five languages, in which objective framing varies with language; solid bars use only English, French and Vietnamese, which share a single framing. Dashes mark the 95th percentile of a permutation null, which is also the smallest disparity the test could have detected; $P$ values are for the framing-controlled comparison. (\textbf {c}) Effect of the assigned persona, resolved by payoff scale: the difference between an agent told it is cooperative and one told it is selfish, at each $\lam $. At $\lam = 0.1$ every model lies left of zero, the instruction moving behaviour opposite to its semantic content. At $\lam \ge 1$ two of the four cross to the right of it. A manipulation that cannot matter decides the sign of one that could, so neither the pooled effect nor any single scale is the whole result}{figure.caption.7}{}}
