\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {What the play is not.} (\textbf  {a}) Cooperation by round with 95\% bootstrap bands. Three of four models decay towards the endgame, consistent with backward induction; Claude is the exception, rising from 0.47 to 0.74. Gemini is the single arm that was told the round count. (\textbf  {b}) Euclidean distance from each model's memory-one fingerprint to every canonical rule, not only to the nearest one, since ``nearest'' hides whether the runner-up was a close second. Markers ring each model's nearest rule. The dashed line is the greatest distance between any two canonical rules in this space, which is the scale on which 0.7--1.0 should be read. (\textbf  {c}) How an archetype label is actually reached. Dark: exactly one of the four canonical rules reproduces the whole trajectory. Mid: several do, and the game does not distinguish them. Grey: none does, and the label is a nearest-neighbour statement from the classifier. The right-hand column gives the mean number of rounds out of ten on which play departs from its own nearest rule.}}{15}{figure.caption.12}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:strategy}{{5}{15}{\textbf {What the play is not.} (\textbf {a}) Cooperation by round with 95\% bootstrap bands. Three of four models decay towards the endgame, consistent with backward induction; Claude is the exception, rising from 0.47 to 0.74. Gemini is the single arm that was told the round count. (\textbf {b}) Euclidean distance from each model's memory-one fingerprint to every canonical rule, not only to the nearest one, since ``nearest'' hides whether the runner-up was a close second. Markers ring each model's nearest rule. The dashed line is the greatest distance between any two canonical rules in this space, which is the scale on which 0.7--1.0 should be read. (\textbf {c}) How an archetype label is actually reached. Dark: exactly one of the four canonical rules reproduces the whole trajectory. Mid: several do, and the game does not distinguish them. Grey: none does, and the label is a nearest-neighbour statement from the classifier. The right-hand column gives the mean number of rounds out of ten on which play departs from its own nearest rule}{figure.caption.12}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{No memory-one rule.}{15}{section*.14}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{A label is mostly not a description.}{15}{section*.15}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The residual is real, and it is not in the class.} (\textbf  {a}) Coverage as the hypothesis class is widened. The classes are nested, so the segments are disjoint and stack to one. The tick is where the same three classes reach on shuffled play, in which the focal agent's own action sequence is permuted in place while its cooperation rate and the opponent's realised sequence are held fixed; coverage to the left of the tick is what the fit buys by chance. (\textbf  {b}) How much history the next move uses. $\Delta $BIC of nested predictors of the next action against a memoryless model, scored on identical rows so the comparison is about history and not sample size. The ring marks each model's selected depth; all four select memory-two. (\textbf  {c}) Why even a fit rarely names a rule. Ten rounds seldom visit all four conditioning states, so rules differing only in an unvisited state are indistinguishable on that game. Bars pool all 4{,}800 agent-games.}}{16}{figure.caption.16}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:residual}{{6}{16}{\textbf {The residual is real, and it is not in the class.} (\textbf {a}) Coverage as the hypothesis class is widened. The classes are nested, so the segments are disjoint and stack to one. The tick is where the same three classes reach on shuffled play, in which the focal agent's own action sequence is permuted in place while its cooperation rate and the opponent's realised sequence are held fixed; coverage to the left of the tick is what the fit buys by chance. (\textbf {b}) How much history the next move uses. $\Delta $BIC of nested predictors of the next action against a memoryless model, scored on identical rows so the comparison is about history and not sample size. The ring marks each model's selected depth; all four select memory-two. (\textbf {c}) Why even a fit rarely names a rule. Ten rounds seldom visit all four conditioning states, so rules differing only in an unvisited state are indistinguishable on that game. Bars pool all 4{,}800 agent-games}{figure.caption.16}{}}
